(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "8.1", title: "Python 2: selection and loops", q: "How do programs make decisions and repeat work?", lessons: [
{ t: "Python recap and diagnostic quiz", o: "I can recall print, input, variables and data types from Year 7.",
  k: ["recap: to go over something learned before", "data type: the kind of value, such as string, integer or float", "casting: converting a value from one data type to another"],
  d: ["Write a Python line that asks for the user's name and stores it.", "name = input('What is your name? ')"],
  s: ["What you already know|`print()` displays output|`input()` asks a question and always gives a string|Variables store values: `score = 0`",
      "Data types and casting|`str`, `int`, `float`|`int(input('Age: '))` turns the answer into a whole number|Without casting, '5' + '5' is '55'",
      "Decisions|>> if age >= 13:|>>     print('Teen')|>> else:|>>     print('Child')"],
  a: "Complete the 15-question diagnostic quiz on your own, then fix the five broken programs. Use your score to choose the recap card you need. Support: work through the recap card for your weakest area with a partner. Stretch: write a program that asks for two numbers and prints the larger one.",
  w: [["What data type does input always give?", "A string."], ["Fix: `age = input('Age: ')` then `print(age + 1)`", "age = int(input('Age: ')) so that 1 can be added."], ["What does `print('3' + '4')` display?", "34"], ["Write a line that stores 9.5 in a variable called `price`.", "price = 9.5"], ["Explain the difference between = and ==.", "= assigns a value to a variable. == compares two values and gives True or False."]],
  x: ["Which topic from the quiz do you most need to practise?", "A named topic."] },
{ t: "if, elif and else in depth", o: "I can write multi-branch selection and choose the right order of conditions.",
  k: ["selection: choosing which code runs, based on a condition", "elif: tests another condition when those above were False", "boundary: the value at which the outcome changes"],
  d: ["A cinema charges children under 12 half price. Write the condition.", "age < 12"],
  s: ["The chain|`if` is tested first|Each `elif` is tested only if everything above was False|`else` runs when nothing matched",
      "Order matters|>> if mark >= 80:|>>     grade = 'A'|>> elif mark >= 60:|>>     grade = 'B'|Put the strictest test first",
      "Test the boundaries|For `>= 80`, test 79, 80 and 81|Most selection bugs are at a boundary|`>` and `>=` differ by exactly one value"],
  a: "Trace the four if-elif-else chains for the given values, then write a program that prints a ticket price from an age: under 5 free, 5 to 15 costs 4, 16 to 64 costs 8, 65 and over costs 5. Support: complete the ticket program with one branch missing. Stretch: add a rule that Tuesdays are half price.",
  w: [["How many branches of one if-elif-else chain can run?", "One."], ["When does the else branch run?", "When none of the conditions above it is True."], ["For the ticket program, what does a 15-year-old pay?", "4"], ["Which three values should you test for the condition `age >= 65`?", "64, 65 and 66."], ["A program tests `mark >= 40` before `mark >= 80`. Explain the bug.", "A mark of 90 matches the first test, so it gets the lower grade and the second test is never reached."]],
  x: ["Write a condition that is True for ages 5 to 15 inclusive, assuming under 5 has already been handled above.", "age <= 15 (or age < 16)."] },
{ t: "Lab: grade calculator and ticket prices", o: "I can build and test programs that use multi-branch selection.",
  k: ["test table: a record of inputs, expected outputs and actual outputs", "expected output: what a correct program should display", "actual output: what the program really displays"],
  d: ["Why write the expected output before running a test?", "So you judge the program against what it should do, not against whatever it prints."],
  s: ["Build|Grade calculator: A, B, C, Ungraded, Invalid|Ticket price program from last lesson|Run after each branch is added",
      "Test table|Columns: input, expected, actual, pass or fail|Include every boundary|Include an invalid value",
      "Check it|Paste your grade calculator into the Python check page of CS Toolkit|Read each FIX message|Fix one at a time"],
  a: "Finish both programs and complete a test table of at least eight rows for the grade calculator. Then check it with the Python check page until every test passes. Support: use the test table with the inputs chosen for you. Stretch: make the calculator also print how many marks are needed for the next grade.",
  w: [["Name the four columns of a test table.", "Input, expected output, actual output, pass or fail."], ["Which inputs test the boundary between Grade B and Grade A?", "79 and 80."], ["What should the grade calculator print for 150?", "Invalid."], ["Your program prints both 'Grade A' and 'Grade B' for 95. What is wrong?", "Separate if statements were used instead of elif, so more than one runs."], ["Explain why an invalid mark should be tested before the grades.", "Otherwise 150 would match `mark >= 80` and be given Grade A."]],
  x: ["How many of the Python check tests passed first time?", "A number out of 8."] },
{ t: "Boolean operators: and, or, not", o: "I can combine conditions using and, or and not.",
  k: ["Boolean: a value that is either True or False", "and: True only when both conditions are True", "or: True when at least one condition is True"],
  d: ["You can go on the ride if you are over 120 cm tall and over 8 years old. Sam is 130 cm and 7. Can Sam ride?", "No. Both conditions must be true."],
  s: ["Three operators|`and`: both must be True|`or`: at least one must be True|`not`: flips True to False",
      "In code|>> if height >= 120 and age >= 8:|>>     print('You can ride')|>> if day == 'Sat' or day == 'Sun':|>>     print('Weekend')",
      "A classic mistake|`if day == 'Sat' or 'Sun':` is always True|Each side of `or` must be a full comparison|A range check: `if mark >= 0 and mark <= 100:`"],
  a: "Complete the truth tables for and, or and not, then predict the output of the eight conditions. Write a program that checks whether a number is between 1 and 10 inclusive. Support: complete the truth tables with the cards. Stretch: write one condition that is True only for a leap year.",
  w: [["When is `A and B` True?", "Only when both A and B are True."], ["When is `A or B` False?", "Only when both A and B are False."], ["`x = 7`. Is `x > 5 and x < 10` True or False?", "True."], ["`x = 12`. Is `x < 5 or x > 10` True or False?", "True."], ["Explain what is wrong with `if colour == 'red' or 'blue':` and correct it.", "'blue' on its own counts as True, so the condition is always True. Write: if colour == 'red' or colour == 'blue':"]],
  x: ["Write a condition that is True when `age` is between 13 and 19 inclusive.", "age >= 13 and age <= 19"] },
{ t: "Nested selection", o: "I can put one decision inside another.",
  k: ["nested: placed inside another structure", "indentation level: how far a line is indented, showing which block it belongs to", "path: one route through the decisions of a program"],
  d: ["'If it is a school day: if it is raining, take the bus; otherwise walk.' How many questions are asked on a Saturday?", "One. The rain question is only asked on a school day."],
  s: ["Nesting|An `if` inside another `if`|The inner one is only reached when the outer one is True|Each level is indented four more spaces",
      "Example|>> if has_ticket == 'yes':|>>     if age >= 18:|>>         print('Enter')|>>     else:|>>         print('Need an adult')|>> else:|>>     print('Buy a ticket')",
      "Nested or `and`?|Use `and` when there is one outcome for 'both true'|Use nesting when different messages are needed at each stage|Count the paths: this example has three"],
  a: "Trace the nested program for four sets of inputs, then write a login program: ask for a username; only if it is correct, ask for the password; give a different message for each failure. Support: add the missing indentation to the part-written program. Stretch: allow a second username with its own password.",
  w: [["What does nested mean?", "Placed inside another structure."], ["How does Python know which `if` an `else` belongs to?", "By its indentation: it lines up with its `if`."], ["In the ticket example, what is printed for has_ticket = 'yes' and age = 15?", "Need an adult."], ["How many paths does the ticket example have?", "Three."], ["Explain when nesting is better than using `and`.", "When each stage needs its own message or action, such as a different message for a wrong username and a wrong password."]],
  x: ["How many spaces is a line indented when it is inside two `if` statements?", "Eight."] },
{ t: "Lab: text adventure, part 1", o: "I can use selection to build a branching story.",
  k: ["branching: splitting into different routes", "decision tree: a diagram of choices and where each leads", "validation: checking that input is acceptable"],
  d: ["A story offers the choices 'left' or 'right' and the user types 'up'. What should the program do?", "Tell the user the choice is not valid."],
  s: ["Plan with a decision tree|Start with one room and two choices|Each choice leads to a new room or an ending|Aim for at least four endings",
      "Code one decision|>> choice = input('Go left or right? ')|>> if choice == 'left':|>>     print('You find a river.')|>> elif choice == 'right':|>>     print('You find a cave.')|>> else:|>>     print('That is not a choice.')",
      "Go deeper|Nest the next choice inside each branch|Use `.lower()` so 'Left' also works|Test every path"],
  a: "Draw a decision tree for your adventure, then code it with at least two levels of choice and four endings. Tick each path on the tree as you test it. Support: extend the starter adventure, which has one level. Stretch: add an item the player can pick up (a variable) that changes a later outcome.",
  w: [["What is a decision tree?", "A diagram of choices and where each one leads."], ["Why use `choice.lower()`?", "So that capital letters do not stop a valid choice from matching."], ["What does the `else` branch do in the example?", "It handles any input that is not one of the allowed choices."], ["Your adventure has two choices at each of two levels. How many endings?", "Four."], ["Explain how you can be sure every ending has been tested.", "Follow each path on the decision tree in turn and tick it off."]],
  x: ["How many paths does your adventure have, and how many have you tested?", "Two numbers."] },
{ t: "for loops and range", o: "I can use a for loop to repeat code a set number of times.",
  k: ["iteration: repeating code", "for loop: a loop that repeats a set number of times", "range: a function that produces a sequence of numbers for a loop"],
  d: ["In Scratch, which block repeats code 10 times?", "repeat 10."],
  s: ["A count-controlled loop|>> for i in range(5):|>>     print('Hello')|Prints Hello five times|The indented lines are the loop body",
      "What range gives|`range(5)` gives 0, 1, 2, 3, 4: it stops before 5|`range(1, 6)` gives 1, 2, 3, 4, 5|`range(0, 10, 2)` gives 0, 2, 4, 6, 8",
      "Using the loop variable|>> for i in range(1, 4):|>>     print(i, 'squared is', i * i)|`i` takes each value in turn"],
  a: "Predict the output of the eight loops, then write loops that: print your name 10 times, count from 1 to 20, count down from 10 to 1, and print the even numbers to 30. Support: predict the first four and run them to check. Stretch: print a triangle of stars using `print('*' * i)`.",
  w: [["What numbers does `range(4)` give?", "0, 1, 2, 3."], ["What numbers does `range(1, 6)` give?", "1, 2, 3, 4, 5."], ["How many times does `for i in range(3, 8):` repeat?", "5 times."], ["Write a loop that prints the numbers 1 to 10.", "for i in range(1, 11):\n    print(i)"], ["Explain why `range(10)` does not include 10.", "range stops before the end value. It starts at 0, so it still gives ten numbers: 0 to 9."]],
  x: ["Write the range that gives 5, 10, 15, 20.", "range(5, 21, 5)"] },
{ t: "Loops with running totals and counts", o: "I can use a loop to total and to count.",
  k: ["running total: a variable that is added to each time round a loop", "counter: a variable that goes up by one each time something happens", "initialise: to give a variable its starting value"],
  d: ["Add up 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 in your head. What did you keep track of as you went?", "15. You kept a running total."],
  s: ["Totalling|>> total = 0|>> for i in range(5):|>>     n = int(input('Number: '))|>>     total = total + n|>> print(total)",
      "Counting|>> count = 0|>> for i in range(10):|>>     mark = int(input('Mark: '))|>>     if mark >= 50:|>>         count = count + 1",
      "The pattern|Initialise before the loop|Update inside the loop|Output after the loop: check the indentation"],
  a: "Trace the totalling loop for the inputs 4, 7, 2, 9, 3. Then write a program that asks for 6 temperatures and prints the total, the average and how many were above 20. Support: complete the trace table with the first two rows filled in. Stretch: also print the highest temperature.",
  w: [["Why is `total = 0` written before the loop and not inside it?", "Inside the loop it would be reset to 0 every time round."], ["Trace: total = 0, then add 4, 7, 2. Give each value of total.", "4, 11, 13."], ["What is the difference between a counter and a running total?", "A counter goes up by 1. A running total goes up by the value of each item."], ["How do you calculate an average after the loop?", "Divide the total by the number of items."], ["The line `print(total)` is indented inside the loop. Explain what happens.", "The total is printed every time round the loop instead of once at the end."]],
  x: ["Name the three steps of the totalling pattern.", "Initialise before, update inside, output after."] },
{ t: "Lab: times-table tester and turtle patterns", o: "I can use for loops to build useful and creative programs.",
  k: ["turtle: a Python module that draws by moving a pen around the screen", "module: extra code that can be imported into a program", "pattern: something that repeats"],
  d: ["What does this print?\n`for i in range(1, 4):`\n`    print(7, 'x', i, '=', 7 * i)`", "7 x 1 = 7, 7 x 2 = 14, 7 x 3 = 21."],
  s: ["Times table|Ask for a number|Loop from 1 to 12|Print each line in the form 7 x 3 = 21",
      "Turtle|>> import turtle|>> for i in range(4):|>>     turtle.forward(100)|>>     turtle.right(90)|Draws a square",
      "Patterns|Change 4 and 90 to draw other shapes: sides x angle = 360|Put a shape loop inside another loop and turn a little each time|That is a nested loop"],
  a: "Write the times-table program and check it on the Python check page of CS Toolkit. Then use turtle to draw a square, a triangle and a hexagon, and combine a shape with a second loop to make a pattern. Support: complete the times-table program with the range missing. Stretch: turn the times table into a tester that asks 10 questions and gives a score.",
  w: [["Which range gives the numbers 1 to 12?", "range(1, 13)"], ["What does `import turtle` do?", "It loads the turtle module so its drawing commands can be used."], ["What angle does the turtle turn at each corner of a hexagon?", "60 degrees."], ["A times table stops at 11. What is the likely bug?", "range(1, 12) was used instead of range(1, 13)."], ["Explain what a nested loop is, using the turtle pattern as an example.", "A loop inside another loop. The inner loop draws one shape; the outer loop repeats it, turning a little each time."]],
  x: ["How many times does the inner line run in a loop of 6 inside a loop of 10?", "60."] },
{ t: "while loops and conditions", o: "I can use a while loop to repeat code until a condition changes.",
  k: ["while loop: a loop that repeats as long as its condition is True", "condition-controlled: stopping because of a condition, not a count", "infinite loop: a loop whose condition never becomes False"],
  d: ["'Keep stirring until the sugar has dissolved.' Do you know in advance how many stirs that takes?", "No. It depends on a condition, not a count."],
  s: ["When to use while|When you do not know how many repeats are needed|The condition is tested before each pass|If it is False at the start, the body never runs",
      "Example|>> count = 1|>> while count <= 3:|>>     print(count)|>>     count = count + 1|Prints 1, 2, 3",
      "Infinite loops|If nothing in the body changes the condition, it never ends|Forgetting `count = count + 1` is the usual cause|Stop a stuck program with the Stop button or Ctrl+C"],
  a: "Predict the output of the six while loops, including one infinite loop that you must fix. Then write a countdown from 10 that ends with 'Lift off' and a loop that doubles a number until it passes 1000. Support: predict the first three and run them. Stretch: count how many doublings it takes to pass one million.",
  w: [["When does a while loop stop?", "When its condition becomes False."], ["What is an infinite loop?", "A loop whose condition never becomes False."], ["Trace: `n = 1`, `while n < 20: n = n * 2`. Give every value of n.", "1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32."], ["When should you choose while instead of for?", "When the number of repeats is not known in advance."], ["A loop is `while lives > 0:` but lives is never changed in the body. Explain what happens and the fix.", "The condition is always True, so the loop never ends. Change lives inside the loop."]],
  x: ["Which loop is condition-controlled and which is count-controlled?", "while is condition-controlled. for is count-controlled."] },
{ t: "Input validation with while", o: "I can use a while loop to keep asking until the input is valid.",
  k: ["validation: checking that input is sensible before it is used", "range check: checking that a number is between two limits", "valid: acceptable"],
  d: ["A program asks for a mark out of 100 and the user types 500. What should happen?", "The program should reject it and ask again."],
  s: ["Why validate?|Users make mistakes|Bad data causes wrong results or crashes|Validation checks that data is sensible, not that it is true",
      "The pattern|>> mark = int(input('Mark: '))|>> while mark < 0 or mark > 100:|>>     print('Must be 0 to 100')|>>     mark = int(input('Mark: '))",
      "Other checks|Presence: `while name == '':`|Length: `while len(password) < 8:`|Choice: `while answer != 'y' and answer != 'n':`"],
  a: "Add validation to three programs: a mark from 0 to 100, a menu choice from 1 to 4, and a password of at least 8 characters that is not 'password'. Check the password program on the Python check page of CS Toolkit. Support: complete the mark validation with the condition missing. Stretch: count the attempts and lock out after three.",
  w: [["What is validation?", "Checking that input is sensible before it is used."], ["Write the condition for a loop that rejects numbers outside 1 to 10.", "number < 1 or number > 10"], ["Why is the input line written twice in the pattern?", "Once to get the first value, and once inside the loop to ask again."], ["Name three types of validation check.", "Range, length, presence (accept choice or type)."], ["A user enters 45 for a mark when they scored 54. Explain why validation does not catch this.", "45 is within the allowed range, so it is valid. Validation checks that data is sensible, not that it is correct."]],
  x: ["Write the condition that rejects a password shorter than 8 characters.", "len(password) < 8"] },
{ t: "Lab: number guessing game", o: "I can combine a while loop, selection and a counter in one program.",
  k: ["random: a module that produces numbers by chance", "attempt: one try", "flag: a variable that records whether something has happened"],
  d: ["In Year 7 you drew a flowchart for a guessing game. Which variables did it need?", "Secret, Guess and Attempts."],
  s: ["A secret number|>> import random|>> secret = random.randint(1, 100)|A whole number from 1 to 100, including both ends",
      "The loop|>> guess = 0|>> attempts = 0|>> while guess != secret:|>>     guess = int(input('Guess: '))|>>     attempts = attempts + 1",
      "Feedback|Inside the loop: `if guess > secret:` print 'Too high'|`elif guess < secret:` print 'Too low'|After the loop: print the number of attempts"],
  a: "Build the guessing game in stages: loop until correct, then add too high and too low, then count attempts. Test with a fixed secret of 37 before switching to random. Support: start from the stage 1 program. Stretch: limit the player to seven attempts and reveal the number if they fail.",
  w: [["What does `random.randint(1, 100)` give?", "A random whole number from 1 to 100 inclusive."], ["Which condition keeps the loop going?", "guess != secret"], ["Where does `attempts = attempts + 1` go?", "Inside the loop, once per guess."], ["Why test with a fixed secret number first?", "So you know the right answer and can check the messages are correct."], ["Explain why seven attempts are always enough for 1 to 100 if you guess the middle each time.", "Each guess halves the possibilities: 100, 50, 25, 13, 7, 4, 2, 1."]],
  x: ["How many attempts did your best game take?", "A number."] },
{ t: "Trace tables for loops", o: "I can trace a loop and predict its output.",
  k: ["trace table: a table recording the value of each variable as a program runs", "dry run: working through code by hand", "iteration: one pass through a loop"],
  d: ["`x = 3`, then `x = x * 2`, then `x = x - 1`. What is x?", "5."],
  s: ["Why trace?|To predict output without a computer|To find exactly where a logic error happens|It is examined at IGCSE",
      "How|One column for each variable and one for output|One row for each change|Test the loop condition before every pass",
      "Example|>> total = 0|>> for i in range(1, 4):|>>     total = total + i|i: 1, 2, 3. total: 0, 1, 3, 6"],
  a: "Complete trace tables for the five programs on the sheet, then state the purpose of each. Use the Pseudocode runner in CS Toolkit to see a trace table being built for a similar algorithm. Support: the first two tables have rows started. Stretch: trace the nested loop program.",
  w: [["What goes in the columns of a trace table?", "Each variable, plus the output."], ["Trace: `total = 0`, `for i in range(1, 5): total = total + i`. Final total?", "10."], ["Trace: `n = 20`, `while n > 5: n = n - 6`. Every value of n?", "20, 14, 8, 2."], ["Trace: `count = 0`, `for i in range(6):`, `if i % 2 == 0: count = count + 1`. Final count?", "3 (for i = 0, 2 and 4)."], ["Explain why the loop variable of `for i in range(1, 4)` never shows 4 in the trace table.", "range stops before 4, so i takes only 1, 2 and 3."]],
  x: ["When do you add a new row to a trace table?", "Whenever a variable changes or something is output."] },
{ t: "Debugging logic errors", o: "I can find and fix logic errors using test data and tracing.",
  k: ["logic error: the program runs but gives the wrong result", "off-by-one error: a loop that runs one time too many or too few", "test data: values chosen to check a program"],
  d: ["A program to add the numbers 1 to 10 prints 45. The correct answer is 55. What might be wrong?", "The loop stops at 9: range(1, 10) instead of range(1, 11)."],
  s: ["Logic errors|There is no error message|The output is wrong|You only notice if you know the expected answer",
      "Common causes|Off by one: `range(1, 10)` when 10 should be included|Wrong operator: `>` instead of `>=`, `and` instead of `or`|A line indented inside or outside the loop by mistake|A variable not initialised or reset in the wrong place",
      "Method|Choose test data with a known answer|Trace or add temporary `print` lines to watch variables|Fix one thing and retest"],
  a: "Find and fix the logic error in each of the six programs. For each, record the test data, expected output, actual output and the fix. Support: the faulty line is highlighted in the first three. Stretch: write a program with a subtle logic error and its fix for a partner.",
  w: [["What is a logic error?", "The program runs but gives the wrong result."], ["What is an off-by-one error?", "A loop that runs one time too many or too few."], ["A range check uses `if mark < 0 and mark > 100:`. What is wrong?", "A number cannot be both below 0 and above 100. It should use or."], ["How can extra `print` lines help with debugging?", "They show the values of variables as the program runs, so you can see where they go wrong."], ["Explain why you need to know the expected output before you can find a logic error.", "Without it you cannot tell that the actual output is wrong."]],
  x: ["Name two common causes of logic errors.", "Any two: off by one, wrong operator, wrong indentation, variable not initialised."] },
{ t: "Lab: fix-the-logic challenge", o: "I can debug unfamiliar programs systematically.",
  k: ["systematic: following an organised method", "hypothesis: an idea about the cause that can be tested", "evidence: results that support or disprove a hypothesis"],
  d: ["What is the first thing to do when a program gives the wrong answer?", "Work out what the right answer should be."],
  s: ["The challenge|Ten programs, each with one logic error|Three levels: bronze, silver, gold|Points for each fix with evidence",
      "Work like a scientist|Hypothesis: 'I think the loop stops too early'|Test: run with data that would show it|Evidence: compare expected and actual",
      "Record|Program number|What was wrong|What you changed|The test that proves it now works"],
  a: "Work through the fix-the-logic programs in order, recording hypothesis, fix and evidence for each. Aim for all bronze and silver. Support: work in a pair on bronze. Stretch: complete gold, where each program has two errors.",
  w: [["What does systematic mean?", "Following an organised method."], ["What is a hypothesis in debugging?", "An idea about the cause of the bug that can be tested."], ["A loop should print 1 to 5 but prints 0 to 4. Give the fix.", "Use range(1, 6) instead of range(5)."], ["A total is always 0 at the end. Suggest the cause.", "total = 0 is inside the loop, so it is reset every time."], ["Explain why guessing and changing lines at random is a poor way to debug.", "It can add new bugs, and even if it works you do not know why, so you cannot avoid the mistake next time."]],
  x: ["How many programs did you fix, and at which level?", "A number and a level."] },
{ t: "Plan: text adventure or quiz engine", o: "I can plan a program that uses selection and both kinds of loop.",
  k: ["specification: a description of what the program must do", "decomposition: breaking a problem into smaller parts", "success criteria: what the finished program must do"],
  d: ["Which loop would you use to ask 5 quiz questions? Which to keep asking until a valid answer is given?", "for; while."],
  s: ["The brief|Extend your text adventure, or build a quiz engine|It must use if-elif-else, a for loop, a while loop and validation|Plan today, build next lesson",
      "Decompose|List the parts: welcome, main loop, scoring or inventory, ending|Decide the variables|Sketch a flowchart or numbered steps",
      "Plan the tests|Normal input for every path|Invalid input|Boundary values where a number decides the outcome"],
  a: "Complete the planning sheet: specification in your own words, the parts of the program, variables, a flowchart or steps for the main loop, and a six-row test plan. Support: use the quiz engine planning frame. Stretch: plan a feature that needs a nested loop.",
  w: [["Which project did you choose?", "Text adventure or quiz engine."], ["List the variables your program needs.", "A suitable list."], ["Where will you use a while loop, and what is its condition?", "A use and a valid condition."], ["Where will you use a for loop?", "A suitable use."], ["Write two tests from your plan with expected results.", "Two tests."]],
  x: ["Which part will you build first, and why?", "A part with a reason."] },
{ t: "Assessed build", o: "I can write, test and debug a program to a specification.",
  k: ["independent: working without help", "comment: a note in code that explains it, starting with #", "test evidence: a record of tests and results"],
  d: ["What should you do after writing every few lines?", "Run and test them."],
  s: ["The task|Build your planned program on your own|Notes and your bug diary are allowed|Save regularly",
      "Order|Core first: the main loop and one decision|Then validation|Then extras",
      "Hand in|The code, with comments|The completed test plan with actual results|One trace table for a loop in your program"],
  a: "Build and test your program. Complete the test plan with actual results and trace one loop from your own code. Support: use the syntax help card. Stretch: add a replay option with a while loop around the whole program.",
  w: [["Give the line numbers where your program uses if-elif-else, for and while.", "Three line numbers."], ["Record one test: input, expected, actual.", "A complete test."], ["Complete a trace table for one loop in your program.", "A correct trace."], ["Describe one logic error you fixed.", "An error and its fix."], ["Which part of the specification is not fully met?", "An honest evaluation."]],
  x: ["How many of your planned tests passed?", "A number."] },
{ t: "Feedback, peer code review and reflection", o: "I can review code, act on feedback and reflect on my learning.",
  k: ["code review: reading someone's code to find problems and suggest improvements", "readable: easy to understand", "feedback: comments that help you improve"],
  d: ["Name two things that make code easy for another person to read.", "Meaningful variable names, comments, consistent layout."],
  s: ["Peer review|Swap programs|Run it, then read it|Give one strength and one suggestion using the review form",
